   "id": "e2-59",
   "name": "Impersonal pronouns"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Impersonal pronouns do not refer to a particular person or thing.\n• it: for weather, time, distance and to introduce an idea: It is raining. It is ten o'clock. It is important to keep the town clean.\n• there: to say that something exists: There is a bin at the junction. There are no trucks today. (there is + singular, there are + plural)\n• one, you, they: for people in general: One should not litter. You never know. They say the council will buy new trucks.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Choose it or there: “___ is dangerous to burn plastic.” “___ are three bins in our street.”",
  "steps": [
   "The first introduces an idea: It is dangerous …",
   "The second says that things exist: There are three bins."
  ],
  "answer": "It … There …",
  "checks": []
 },
